# Manuscript PDF page 51

                                   T H E H O N DA



    I went back to the email. We’ve been watching your work. How good it
felt. How I’d read it three times. How I’d never sent a kid in Soundview
an EP I’d finished a week ago, in case he didn’t like the second half.
    The video ended. The next one lined up. I didn’t let it.
    I picked up my phone and opened the scan Sofía had sent, the one
I’d looked at four times already. The Calm Index page. Atypical response
to ambient stimulus: hand-tapping, vocalizing, agitation during rest periods.
In the margin, in blue ballpoint: Kids need to MOVE.
    I’d been looking at it all week as evidence of what Aperture did to
the block. I hadn’t looked at it as evidence of what it had written about
me.
    A woman with a clipboard had watched a six-year-old tap a desk leg
during quiet time, and she’d had a name for it. A different name from
the one in the Honda, and a different reason, but the same kid, the
same hand, the same Tuesday. One system looked at that hand and
wrote disruption. The other looked at it and, nineteen years late, wrote
infrastructure.
    I didn’t feel relief. People think you do. It was more like standing on
a floor and feeling it tilt, because if there was a word for it, then twenty-
five years of doing the math with the wrong numbers meant every vago
I’d ever been called had been a bad answer to a different question.
    My father used to say it. Vago. Lazy. Not cruel. Said the way you’d
say wet about a day it had rained. Over and over, until it became fur‐
niture.
    I sat there in the gray, with a hot laptop and a warm coin on the
nightstand, and I thought: I need to say this out loud to someone who
won’t fix it.
    So I texted Yari.



